", "Prediction markets give Booker only about a 3% win probability", "The only recent polls, from Democratic-leaning sponsors, show Barr ahead by 9-11 points"], "rationale": "Kentucky's deep Republican lean, Barr's dominant fundraising, unanimous expert ratings, and market pricing all point to a comfortable Republican hold of McConnell's open seat, even allowing for a midterm environment that has tilted toward Democrats.", "watch": "A decisive national Democratic wave or unusually high turnout in Louisville and Lexington could narrow the margin, though probably not enough to put the seat in play.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 54 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Mitch McConnell (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kentucky's open Senate seat, vacated by retiring Mitch McConnell, sits in a state that has leaned roughly fifteen points Republican in recent cycles. Andy Barr, a veteran congressman, enters the fall with a large financial advantage, having raised about twelve million dollars and drawn substantial outside backing. Charles Booker, a former state representative from Louisville, has run an active campaign focused on cost-of-living issues but has raised far less and attracted minimal outside support.\n\nThe only recent polling, conducted by Democratic-leaning firms, showed Barr ahead by nine to eleven points. Every major race rating lists the contest as Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Booker roughly a three percent chance. The race's single televised debate in late October produced sharp exchanges over inflation, Iran, and immigration, but no clear sign the contest had narrowed. Barring an unexpected shift, Kentucky is expected to remain in Republican hands.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0216, "margin": -12.87}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0168, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0143, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0384, "ma